#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:6
msgid "Inheritance from Rust's Perspective"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:9
msgid "// Copyright 2025 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:10
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:11
msgid "// Data\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:17
msgid "// Concrete behavior\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:24
msgid "// Abstract behavior\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:29
msgid "// Instanced behavior\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:41
msgid ""
"From Rust's perspective, one where Inheritance was never there, introducing "
"inheritance would look like muddying the water between types and traits."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:44
msgid "A type is a concrete piece of data and its associated behavior."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:46
msgid "A trait is abstract behavior that must be implemented by a type."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:48
msgid ""
"A class is a combination of data, behavior, and overrides to that behavior."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:50
msgid ""
"Coming from Rust, an inheritable class looks like a type that is also a "
"trait."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:52
msgid "This is not an upside, as we can no longer reason about concrete types."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:54
msgid ""
"Without being able to separate the two, it becomes difficult to reason about "
"generic behavior vs concrete specifics, because in OOP these two concepts "
"are tied up in each other."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/from-oop-to-rust/switch-perspective.md:58
msgid ""
"The convenience of flat field access and DRY in type definitions is not "
"worth the loss in specificity between writing code that delineates between "
"behavior and data."
msgstr ""
